# Python Port Plan — Step 06: The Logger

## Goal

Port `week1_baseline/ruby/06_the_logger` to
`week1_baseline/python/06_the_logger`. This step adds `Boukensha::Logger` —
a file logger that writes one structured JSON-Lines file per agent session
to `.boukensha/sessions/<session-id>.jsonl` — and wires `Agent` to call it
at every phase (iteration, prompt, tool_call, tool_result, response,
turn_end, and raw provider responses when debug mode is on) instead of
`puts`-ing directly to the console. Also adds module-level global state on
the `Boukensha` module itself (a memoized `Config` singleton, a dormant
`quiet`/`loud` toggle, and a `debug` toggle that actually gates the logger's
`raw` event), and makes tool-dispatch errors non-fatal — `Agent` now
rescues exceptions from `Registry#dispatch` and feeds an `"ERROR: ..."`
string back to the model as the tool result instead of crashing the loop.

**⚠️ Same safety profile as step 5.** `example.py` still runs a real,
multi-turn agentic loop against the live Anthropic API. New this step: it
also writes a real JSONL file into the **shared** `.boukensha/sessions/`
directory (used by both Ruby and Python runs — confirmed a Ruby-generated
session file, `.boukensha/sessions/20260726T180811Z-0a3d6b44.jsonl`, is
already committed in this repo from an earlier Ruby test run, in commit
`b92eefa`). Running the Python example will add a new file there too.

